1
00:00:00,430 --> 00:00:03,610
When my mum calls twice, I always wonder
what I missed.

2
00:00:04,070 --> 00:00:06,570
Her question is simple: where are my
glasses?

3
00:00:07,070 --> 00:00:08,770
But I can’t see the kitchen from here.

4
00:00:09,310 --> 00:00:09,890
I’m Daniel.

5
00:00:10,370 --> 00:00:11,830
This is Evelyn’s story.

6
00:00:14,390 --> 00:00:16,190
The hard part isn’t the question.

7
00:00:16,690 --> 00:00:19,550
It’s losing the little bit of context that
came before it.

8
00:00:20,090 --> 00:00:24,130
Threadline brings that context back, without
asking Mum to learn another app.

9
00:00:26,370 --> 00:00:26,930
Let me show you.

10
00:00:27,490 --> 00:00:28,390
This is Threadline.

11
00:00:28,870 --> 00:00:30,950
I’m opening a kitchen example in the
Library.

12
00:00:31,390 --> 00:00:33,290
The clip is generated, but the next

13
00:00:33,350 --> 00:00:35,230
response comes from a real request.

14
00:00:36,530 --> 00:00:38,150
I press Ask Gemini again.

15
00:00:38,690 --> 00:00:40,990
Threadline looks at the frames, describes
what it

16
00:00:41,030 --> 00:00:43,010
can see, and chooses a next step.

17
00:00:43,530 --> 00:00:46,650
We wait for that response, instead of
replacing it with a script.

18
00:00:54,350 --> 00:00:56,190
And she can ask in her own language.

19
00:00:56,770 --> 00:00:59,530
Here, I ask in Nepali: where are my glasses?

20
00:01:09,210 --> 00:01:11,990
Gemini answers in Nepali, beside the same
image.

21
00:01:12,410 --> 00:01:13,470
The words change.

22
00:01:13,810 --> 00:01:15,490
The support stays personal.

23
00:01:18,450 --> 00:01:20,650
Now I ask, where are the glasses?

24
00:01:21,230 --> 00:01:23,850
The answer stays beside the scene, so we can
check it.

25
00:01:24,290 --> 00:01:26,289
That’s the difference between a confident
guess

26
00:01:26,550 --> 00:01:28,310
and help we can actually inspect.

27
00:01:30,390 --> 00:01:32,730
Then there’s what happened before the camera
was there.

28
00:01:33,250 --> 00:01:35,450
Here, I search Memory for the blue mug.

29
00:01:35,950 --> 00:01:39,250
Evelyn’s own words appear: she likes her tea
by the window.

30
00:01:39,730 --> 00:01:41,610
I can open the source and see when she said
it.

31
00:01:41,990 --> 00:01:44,510
Context, without making her repeat herself.

32
00:01:47,450 --> 00:01:49,410
I can’t be beside Mum every minute.

33
00:01:50,090 --> 00:01:53,730
But a forgotten detail shouldn’t have to
become a whole lost afternoon.

34
00:01:54,530 --> 00:01:55,070
Threadline.

35
00:01:55,670 --> 00:01:56,590
A little context.

36
00:01:57,110 --> 00:01:57,890
A little closer.
